{"text":"With a deterministic positive and K uniform negatives drawn with replacement from A_u = catalog minus complete training history, ordinary cross-entropy over these K+1 entries is a candidate-set discrimination objective, not an importance-corrected full-catalog softmax approximation. Implementation consequence: writing z=s/τ, an importance estimate of the partition over {positive}∪A_u is exp(z_pos)+(|A_u|/K)Σexp(z_neg). Implement this by adding log(|A_u|/K) to negative logits only. Excluded historical items have zero sampling probability, so this cannot recover the unrestricted catalog partition. Retain repeated negatives with their multiplicity; deduplicating changes the estimator unless multiplicities or inclusion probabilities are accounted for. Temperature changes gradients and concentration; apply sampling corrections after s/τ.

[gSASRec](https://github.com/asash/gsasrec) uses more negatives and generalized BCE, with a calibration parameter controlling positive weighting; it is not sampled-softmax CE. [eSASRec](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.06450v1) combines SASRec’s shifted-sequence objective, gated pre-norm LiGR blocks with SwiGLU, and sampled softmax; mixed negatives are optional. Consequently, eSASRec gains cannot be attributed solely to stronger discrimination: architecture, loss, sampling, and calibration require separate ablations.

[Correcting the LogQ Correction, RecSys 2025](https://arxiv.org/html/2507.09331v1) explicitly separates the always-present positive from sampled negatives. Its proposed surrogate uses a negative-only, importance-corrected log-sum-exp and a detached uncertainty weight, approximately 1−P(positive); it is not ordinary corrected candidate CE. Uniform logQ subtraction cancels when applied equally to every candidate, but the negative-only |A_u|/K partition scaling does not. Label the implemented objective precisely, specify duplicate handling and sampling support, and compare losses at matched negative counts and temperatures before explaining validation gains."}
